+| Xbox / keyboard button glyphs | yes, with the mods folder | `button_prompts = auto` switches by the last device: Xbox glyphs for a pad, keyboard glyphs (drawn with the browser's fonts) after keyboard use; gated against native. PlayStation glyphs for Sony pads: later. Key labels follow the keyboard layout (gate T `rt10gate`: US and AZERTY labels; default since rt12) |
+| HD output (the scaled frame shown at the screen's real resolution) | yes, default (`?hdout=0` = off) | gated at 2560x1440: visibly sharper, fps unchanged, about +34 MB VRAM |
+| Ultrawide / any window shape (Hor+) | yes, default (`?fill=0` = 16:9 box) | gated at 32:9 (5120x1440): wider view, HUD kept 16:9, movies / loading screens pillarboxed, as native; the wider view draws about 22% more, so about 15% lower fps than 16:9 |
+| Saves | yes | in this site's storage; import / export on the start screen (before Start) as the native savedata folders (.zip download, or a .zip / folder you pick): byte-identical round trip, a browser export loads in the native port |
+| Create-a-Park (companion + live link) | no | later phase |
+| Frame rate | about 49 fps at fps = 60 (16:9), about 42 fps at 32:9 (default rt25: upload staging, constant-buffer hints, vertex-window skip, poll-wait locks, exact sleeps on 12+ core machines) | gates `rt20gate169` / `rt20gate329` (3+3 medians, +18% / +15% over rt12w); default checks `rt22def3` 48.9 / 42.0, `rt25def` 49.1 / 42.4; default u1 (thp8-unified, 2026-10-24) one boot each vs rt25 on the same page: 47.5 / 55.8 vs 50.1 / 51.0 at 16:9 (`wu1`, `wu2`: the usual two-mode spread, no regression seen). Sleep mode 3 uses one extra CPU core; laptops (under 12 logical cores) keep mode 0. See Known issues |
+| Memory | browser build host heap peaks about 825-835 MB of its 1024 MB with preset high + HD packs (16:9 and 32:9), about 190 MB headroom; GPU textures about 1.3 GB (16:9) to 1.7 GB (32:9) | |
+
+## Known issues
+
+**Brief hitch when a new area streams in (Chrome's GPU process, parked).** The first time the game enters a new scene
+(for example skating out of the garage into the yard), one or two frames can take about 150-350 ms. The executor
+already takes our own work off that path: shaders are translated on helper workers, pipelines are created
+asynchronously and a draw is skipped until its pipeline is ready, and every pipeline seen in an earlier session is
+recompiled in the background at start (OPFS cache `webcache/wgs_cache.ndjson`), so a second visit is clean. What is
+left happens inside the browser: the device worker's diagnostics (`[gpu] slow fence` lines) show the worker idle while
+a submitted fence takes 150-260 ms to complete, i.e. Chrome's GPU process or the driver is busy (new pipeline
+compiles on its own threads, shader module validation, first-use uploads). Our code cannot shorten that wait, so this
+is parked; it affects scene entry only, not steady in-level frame time. Measured on desktop Chrome, NVIDIA, 2026-10-11.
+
+**Frame rate on the current Chrome: about 42-44 fps at fps = 60.** In the free-skate garage at fps = 60, desktop
+Chrome 155 on this PC runs the browser build at about 42-44 fps (frames over 33 ms: 5-35 per 10 s). Earlier boots
+(Chrome 154, and two boots on 2026-10-13) reached about 54 fps; that faster result has not come back in 30+ boots
+since, and it is not explained by the game's data cache, by the WebAssembly compiler tier, by Chrome's field
+trials or by CPU clock speed (all tested, 2026-10-13/14). The GPU is mostly idle (about 1.5 ms of GPU work per
+frame); the limit is CPU-side work in the browser.
+
+## Measuring changes (A/B rules)
+
+Boot-to-boot spread is large (see above), so one boot per side says nothing. A change is judged on the **median of at least 3
+boots per side**, run interleaved (A B B A ...) in a quiet slot: no other heavy work on the PC, total CPU under 15%
+before each boot, and every boot a fresh browser with the same settings. Scene-entry hitches (frames over 50 ms after
+a level starts) are reported next to the frame rate. A feature becomes default-on only after it also passes a visual
+check against the native port in the same scene.
+
+## Future (not started)
+
+**Mod browser, in the launcher and in the web build.** One place to browse, enable and order mods packs, in the
+P8Plus launcher and inside the browser page. Not started on purpose: how mods would be published and hosted comes
+first. Game-derived content (repacked game files, texture packs made from the game, lifted code) is never hosted:
+it is only served from the user's own PC, read from their own mods folder, the same way the game data is.
+
+**Sharing parks and CAS looks.** Created parks and Create-a-Skater looks would be shared only as small layout files
+(which pieces, where, which colours), never as game-derived data: the receiving PC rebuilds the park or the look
+locally from its own game files.
diff --git a/web/host/audio-worklet.js b/web/host/audio-worklet.js
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..03ac0773
--- /dev/null
+++ b/web/host/audio-worklet.js
@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+// audio-worklet.js -- plays cellAudio's output ring (libs/audio/cellAudio_web.inc.h) from shared wasm memory.
+// Ring: +0 version, +4 capacity (frames, power of two), +8 sample rate, +12 channels (2), +16 write_pos,
+//       +20 read_pos, +24 underruns, +28 running, +32 interleaved float32 stereo.
+//
+// Starvation is silence, never noise: when the game cannot keep the ring filled (a slow frame rate), the player
+// stops, outputs zeros and consumes nothing until PREBUFFER frames are queued again, then fades back in. Playing
+// whatever partial quantum happened to be there, between zero-filled gaps, was heard as constant garbled audio.
+// A writer that lapped the reader (more than the ring holds) is resynchronised to the newest PREBUFFER frames.
+const PREBUFFER = 2048;          // ~43 ms at 48 kHz
+const FADE = 128;                // frames of fade-in after a restart
+class Ps3RingPlayer extends AudioWorkletProcessor {
+  constructor(opts) {
+    super();
+    const { memory, ring } = opts.processorOptions;
+    this.i32 = new Int32Array(memory, ring, 8);
+    this.cap = this.i32[1] >>> 0;
+    this.data = new Float32Array(memory, ring + 32, this.cap * 2);
+    this.volume = 1;
+    this.playing = false;
+    this.fade = 0;
+    this.port.onmessage = (e) => { if (e.data.volume !== undefined) this.volume = e.data.volume; };
+  }
+  process(_in, outputs) {
+    const out = outputs[0], L = out[0], R = out[1] || out[0], n = L.length;
+    const w = Atomics.load(this.i32, 4) >>> 0;
+    let r = Atomics.load(this.i32, 5) >>> 0;
+    let avail = (w - r) >>> 0;
+    if (avail > this.cap) { r = (w - Math.min(PREBUFFER, this.cap)) >>> 0; avail = (w - r) >>> 0; }   // lapped
+    if (!this.playing && avail >= Math.min(PREBUFFER, this.cap >>> 1)) { this.playing = true; this.fade = 0; }
+    if (!this.playing || avail < n) {
+      L.fill(0); if (R !== L) R.fill(0);
+      if (this.playing) {                       // ran dry mid-stream: stop until the ring refills
+        this.playing = false;
+        if (Atomics.load(this.i32, 7)) Atomics.add(this.i32, 6, 1);
+      }
+      Atomics.store(this.i32, 5, r | 0);
+      return true;
+    }
+    const mask = this.cap - 1, v = this.volume;
+    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++, r++) {
+      const g = this.fade < FADE ? (this.fade++ / FADE) * v : v;
+      const k = (r & mask) * 2;
+      L[i] = this.data[k] * g; R[i] = this.data[k + 1] * g;
+    }
+    Atomics.store(this.i32, 5, r | 0);
+    return true;
+  }
+}
+registerProcessor("ps3-ring-player", Ps3RingPlayer);
